<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wakhan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wakhan Corridor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wakhan is a high, narrow valley in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, inhabited mainly by Wakhi and Kyrgyz herders. A route of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, it was ruled by local mirs under Badakhshan. In 1893 and 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> set it as a strip of Afghan territory, the Wakhan Corridor, separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wakhan.jpg" alt="Wakhan" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wakhan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
